      "report_date": "2026-10-06",
      "title": "CrocoRat Adds a New Twist to ClickFix with DNS Payload Delivery",
      "summary": "CrocoRat is a previously undocumented remote access trojan and cryptocurrency stealer delivered through a ClickFix lure on a typo-squatted domain that tricks victims into pasting a command into the Windows Run dialog. The command queries a DNS TXT record, which returns the next-stage PowerShell that downloads a portable Python bundle to C:\\ProgramData\\App and sets up a scheduled task for persistence. The launcher compares USERDOMAIN and COMPUTERNAME to decide the path: domain-looking machines receive only the RAT, while standalone systems also receive a browser-focused stealer and a wallet and seed phrase collector. Researchers could not decrypt the final RAT payload because the attacker's C2 key was withheld, and the operator changed filenames, keys, and infrastructure between builds. Attribution points only loosely to a Russian-speaking developer. Defenders should focus on the behavioral chain, including ClickFix-driven PowerShell, DNS-based staging, and host-aware branching, rather than on specific indicators.",
      "completeness": "Medium",
      "threats": [
