      "report_date": "2026-10-06",
      "title": "Up a Creek Without a Command Line: Mapping an Akira Ransomware Attack",
      "summary": "Huntress investigated an Akira ransomware attack on an organization where its agent was installed only after the attacker had already compromised the environment, so EDR telemetry was limited. Researchers reconstructed the attack from Windows Registry Shellbags, Event Logs, and Akira log files. The attacker entered via RDP from a non-customer workstation, stopped Bitdefender services, and ran procdump, likely to dump lsass.exe credentials. They then deployed the open-source GOST tunneling tool for persistence and used Rclone, which is associated with data exfiltration. Defenders should monitor for access from unknown workstations and watch directories such as C:\\PerfLogs for suspicious executables.",
      "completeness": "Medium",
      "threats": [
